Implemented System: External ARM-Based Hardware Security Module (Raspberry Pi 3) + Isolated Point-to-Point Channel + Ephemeral Key Rotation
Host Platform: Dell G15 5520 (x86_64 Architecture, 24 GB RAM)
Conceptual Classification: Out-of-Band Hardware Security Module (OOB-HSM) with Physical Presence Verification (PPV) and Forward Secrecy.

1. Technical Nomenclature of the Architecture

The developed architecture goes beyond a simple "password USB stick" and implements four established security engineering standards:
• Dedicated External HSM (Dedicated Hardware Security Module): The Raspberry Pi 3 acts as a physically separated cryptographic coprocessor, operating on a silicon architecture free of hidden out-of-band management (ARM without Intel ME).
• Isolated Point-to-Point Channel (Dedicated Air-Gapped Link): The direct Ethernet cable connection (10.99.99.1 ↔ 10.99.99.2) with no external routing creates an exclusive private bus between the key authority and the host computer.
• Physical Presence Verification (PPV): The push-button wired to the GPIO pins (Pins 9 and 11) requires mechanical human actuation to authorize cryptographic key release, blocking automated requests from malicious software on the laptop.
• Session-Based Ephemeral Rotation (Session-Based Key Rotation / Forward Secrecy): Automatic replacement of the access key upon every unlock ensures no credential is ever reused across distinct sessions.

2. Why This Architecture Is Not an Absolute Solution (The Predictive Nature of the Host)

Even with a hardened external HSM and an isolated channel operating flawlessly, the solution encounters a physical, deterministic limitation of modern computing (Von Neumann Architecture): the host system (laptop) is predictable in its execution flow.

2.1. The Inevitable Destination of the Key in System RAM

• Separation Between Storage and Execution: The Raspberry Pi 3 stores and unwraps the key securely, but it lacks the bus bandwidth and processing power to execute the host's Virtual Machine or operating system.
• Mandatory Delivery: For the Dell G15 to read the encrypted volume (LUKS2 or VeraCrypt), the decryption key must traverse the dedicated cable and be loaded into the host's main RAM.
• The Host Blind Spot: The moment the key enters the Dell G15's RAM, it leaves the secure jurisdiction of the Raspberry Pi 3 and enters the physical domain governed by the x86 processor and motherboard chipset (Ring 0 and Ring -3 / Intel ME).

2.2. Forensic Predictability in System RAM

An attacker with access to the lowest hardware tier (Ring -3) or the kernel (Ring 0) does not need to guess where the key resides in RAM. Cryptographic keys exhibit highly predictable traits for memory forensics tools:
• High Entropy: Standard data (text, images, binaries) contains repetitive patterns. A 256-bit or 512-bit AES key displays purely random distribution (maximum entropy), standing out immediately across 24 GB of RAM.
• Mathematical Signature (AES Key Schedule): To accelerate encryption, the CPU expands the original key into a table of round keys in RAM following a rigid mathematical relationship. 
Scanning algorithms (such as aeskeyfind) locate this mathematical structure within seconds.

3. Strategies Implemented to Hinder Extraction and Mitigate Damage

Because preventing the key from passing through RAM during active laptop use is physically impossible, the architecture enforces four containment layers to make extraction extremely difficult and limit the blast radius if it occurs:

3.1. Temporal Containment via Session Rotation (Forward Secrecy)

• Mechanism: The exact moment the vault is unlocked, the script writes the next session's key and deletes the current key from the disk header.
• Practical Effect: An attacker's window of opportunity is strictly confined to the duration of that single active session. If the key is extracted from RAM today, it is already mathematically invalid for unlocking the vault tomorrow.

3.2. Active Memory Destruction (Dead Man's Switch)

• Mechanism: The closing script unmounts the encrypted volume, flushes system page caches (drop_caches on Linux or /wipe in VeraCrypt), and overwrites environment variables in memory.
• Practical Effect: Prevents residual keys from lingering in RAM after the Virtual Machine is closed or while the system sits idle.

3.3. Elimination of Disk Leakage (Zero Swap)

• Mechanism: Disabling the paging file (swapoff / disabling pagefile.sys), taking advantage of the Dell G15's 24 GB RAM headroom.
• Practical Effect: Guarantees the operating system never copies RAM pages containing the active key onto the internal SSD during memory spikes.

3.4. Obfuscation via Cascading Encryption

• Mechanism: Using multiple chained algorithms (such as AES-Twofish-Serpent in VeraCrypt) or dual-layer encryption (encrypted Host + encrypted VM).
• Practical Effect: Forces the system to maintain multiple independent keys of varying sizes and mathematical structures scattered across RAM, breaking automated tools that only scan for standard single-AES key schedules.

4. Executive Summary

The Dedicated Physical HSM with Isolated Channel and GPIO Button definitively solves data-at-rest protection, eliminates reliance on the motherboard's internal TPM chip, 
and blocks silent remote attacks. While the deterministic nature of the x86 architecture makes the key's passage through RAM unavoidable during active execution, 
ephemeral per-session rotation combined with immediate memory sanitization neutralizes the persistence of any capture, delivering enterprise/military-grade security on consumer hardware.
